### MSG-8g — DM read falls back across stored key eras; MCP plaintext wording corrected

- `e6bdab745` fix(messaging): `aitbc.dm_client` gains `DmDecryptError` (a `ValueError` subclass raised only for the SealedBox `CryptoError` case, same message as before) and `open_dm_with_eras()`, which tries the stored secret list newest to oldest and returns the first body that opens. Only a decrypt failure advances to the next era; a malformed ciphertext, non-UTF-8 payload or `aitbc-dm-v1` header mismatch raises on the spot — a tampered or misrouted message is never retried against older keys. When no era opens, one `ValueError` names only the tried count — no key, era or ciphertext material. `dm read` (CLI, via `_dm_key_eras`) and `MessagingClient.dm_read` (SDK) both walk the wallet's full `dm-keys` era list through the shared helper, so a DM sealed to a superseded `dm_key` still opens after `dm keygen --rotate` (MSG-VERIFY-1 G2). An explicit SDK `secret_key=` keeps its single-key meaning; corrupt stores still raise `DmStoreCorruptError` untouched; the empty-store and single-era paths are unchanged.
- Same commit, docs: the "MCP layer never sees plaintext" claim was wrong — the generated `aitbc_messaging_dm_*` tools shell out to `aitbc` on the resolved host (local subprocess or `ssh` via `_run_remote`) and return its stdout in the tool result, so DM plaintext transits the MCP server process and reaches a remote MCP client; key material is still read only on the host the CLI runs on. `docs/design/msg8-direct-messages.md` §5 and `docs/features/agent-messaging-paths.md` now say so (G4), and the design's sender-copy section records that Phase 1 implements no sender copy at all (recommendation (a) deferred — G3).
- Same commit, tests: `TestOpenDmWithEras` kernel coverage in `apps/blockchain-node/tests/test_messaging_dm.py`; CLI-era coverage (`TestDmReadEras`) in `cli/tests/test_messaging_dm.py`; SDK-era coverage in `packages/py/aitbc-sdk/tests/test_messaging_dm.py` — rotation round trip on both read paths, tried-count reporting without key/ciphertext material, header-failure no-fall-through (call-count spy), tampered-ciphertext-across-all-eras, single/empty/corrupt store parity and the explicit-`secret_key` single-attempt path.


### EXPL-STATS-2 — network-stats: _chain_db_path() OSError answers 503, not 500

An `OSError` raised by `_chain_db_path()` itself (e.g. `PermissionError`
on the data directory) previously escaped `api_network_stats` before the
`try`, surfacing as an anonymous 500 while every other failure of the
route answers 503. The path lookup now runs inside the `try`, so the
same failure answers 503 "network stats unavailable" with no path or
exception text in the body.


### OLDFORM-COUNTER-1 — count old-form signed bodies on replay-guarded routes

`blockchain_replay_guard_requests_total{endpoint, form}` counts signed
bodies on `/bridge/lock` and the cross-chain lock routes by form
(`new_form`, `legacy_accepted`, `legacy_refused`), so the
`BRIDGE_LOCK_REQUIRE_REPLAY_GUARD` flip can rest on observed traffic
rather than a code census. `legacy_accepted` increments only after the
signature verifies — unauthenticated traffic cannot fake live old-form
signers — and `legacy_refused` counts inside the flag's 400 path. First
`legacy_accepted` per endpoint per process also logs one WARNING.
Counter is process-local, resets on rpc restart, and is not live until a
separate rollout.



### CALLERS-NEWFORM-1 — callers can submit replay-guarded signed bodies

The callers that could not send `issued_at`/`nonce` now can, while old-form
bodies still work where the guard flags are off. `aitbc bridge lock
--signature` gains `--issued-at`/`--nonce` (both-or-neither; a warning fires
on the old form, which enforced nodes refuse with 400). MCP `bridge_lock`
takes optional `issued_at`/`nonce`. MCP `create_cross_chain_swap` and
`create_cross_chain_bridge` take `signature`/`issued_at`/`nonce` — they
previously sent unsigned bodies and have answered 403 since signature
verification landed; `create_cross_chain_bridge` also gains the `sender`
field its body never carried. The dormant coordinator `BridgeClientAdapter`
and trading `lock_escrow` wrappers forward both fields to
`BridgeClient.lock()`. Server-side guard behavior, flags, nonce TTLs
unchanged. Repo-only: nothing is deployed; CLI/MCP/coordinator rollout needs
a separate operator step.


### TOOL-MCP-2 — close the three residual MCP tooling gaps

- `aitbc system env-names --process` reads the running process environment
  (`/proc/<MainPID>/environ`): names only by default, `--names` set/unset,
  `declared_only`/`process_only` drift flags against the declared env files,
  and `--value NAME` for ONE non-deny-listed variable (deny-list + 200-char
  cap; refusals name the rule, never the value).
- `aitbc prometheus metric --name <m>` reads ONE named metric from the
  node's own `:9009/metrics` exposition (exact match; integer counters stay
  integers; loopback-only unless `--allow-remote`, which the generated MCP
  tools never expose). `aitbc_prometheus_metric` is a read-only MCP tool.
- Gap 3 (counter-to-journal pairing) resolved as a documented recipe —
  `docs/ops/counter-to-journal-recipe.md` — built from `prometheus metric`
  and `system logs --since/--grep`; no new tool.

