### SEC-19: dependency-scan.sh no longer reinstalls safety or mutates the project venv (2026-10-14)

The script still ran `pip install safety bandit pip-audit` into the repo
venv on every run — on a SEC-15-cleaned host that reinstalls the removed
safety/Authlib chain, and the install always mutated the live venv's
dependency set. The script now runs pip-audit only, resolved in order:
pip-audit on PATH, an existing `AITBC_SCAN_TOOLS_VENV`, else a throwaway
venv (env-var path or `mktemp -d`) removed on exit. It never creates
`./venv` (that is setup-python-venv.sh's job) and never installs into it.
Exit behaviour is unchanged — a findings report prints a warning and the
script still exits 0; it documents itself as a report, not a gate.
SECURITY.md and the dev-tier tool lists in install-profiles.sh /
requirements-test.txt no longer name the removed package. New tests run a
copy of the script under stub shims and assert no safety invocation, no
project-venv install, tools-venv removal and clean exit, plus bash -n and
no-safety static guards.


### ETH-18: a young pending release logs below ERROR, not 'needs review' (2026-10-14)

During ETH-16's live withdrawal the hub withdraw monitor broadcast the
release and, 30 s later, logged ERROR "still unverifiable — needs review"
while the row stayed `releasing`; the next poll confirmed the receipt and
completed it. The tx had simply been mined below
`BRIDGE_RELEASE_CONFIRMATIONS` depth — a normal delay, not an incident.

`_release_nonce_outcome` now splits the nonce view into `nonce_consumed`
(the nonce was taken without a qualifying receipt — a real anomaly that
keeps ERROR, per ETH-15's documented intent) and `nonce_pending` (the tx
sits in the pending pool — routine). In `_resume_unfinished_releases`, a
`chain_view`/`nonce_pending` "unknown" younger than
`ETH_RELEASE_ABSENCE_GRACE_SECONDS` logs at INFO with the reason and age;
`verify_error`, `nonce_consumed` and aged rows keep ERROR, and the ERROR
line now names the reason. The sell-escrow inflight check applies the same
split at WARNING (its unknown path does persist `needs_review`). Log level
and log content only — no outcome, status, refund or parking decision
moved. Watcher note: `AITBCJournalErrors` counts `[ERROR]` lines per unit,
so routine young-release delays no longer feed it; real anomalies do.


### REPO-1: file-info denylist extension + env-names case + verify DB containment + OPS-4 nginx example (2026-10-15)

`system file-info --field`'s denylist only caught the literal `*_key`
spellings — `privatekey`, `api-key`, `pass_phrase` and friends all
slipped through. The check now normalises each candidate (strips `-`
and `_`, lowercases) and additionally refuses the exact names
privatekey, apikey, secretkey, passphrase, passwd, credential and
credentials, plus any name ending in `key` whose prefix is
private/secret/api/auth/signing/access. `pubkey`, `monkey` and the
rest of the `*key` tail stay allowed; a denied value still never
reaches stdout or the log.

`system env-names --names` uppercased each requested name before
matching, so a lowercase query could never match its own variable —
environment names are case-sensitive and are now reported exactly as
given.

`exchange verify`'s help examples pointed at
`/var/lib/aitbc/exchange.db`; the real path is
`/var/lib/aitbc/data/exchange/exchange.db`. `--db`/`--bridge-db` now
must resolve under `/var/lib/aitbc/` on top of the existing
absolute/no-`..`/no-symlink checks — the wrapper can no longer be
aimed at arbitrary SQLite files elsewhere on the host.

`examples/nginx/nginx-aitbc.conf.example` gains the OPS-4 signed-order
block verbatim from the hub vhost: `/api/internal/` 404s, the six
exact-match `/api/*` exchange locations are method-pinned via
`limit_except`, rate-limited, capped at 16 kB bodies, and proxy with
`X-API-Key`/`Authorization` stripped. The file's "every location
exists in production" claim stays true.

Tests: denylist accept/refuse parametrisation, env-names
case-sensitivity, verify prefix accept/refuse/boundary cases — the
system and exchange CLI suites, ruff, mypy ratchet, check-cli-docs,
check-mcp-cli-drift and check-c901-ratchet all pass.

Commits: `8a0218e8d` (denylist + env-names + verify containment +
nginx block + tests + MCP regen).


### REPO-2: fee defaults track DEFAULT_TX_FEE_AIT + verifier eth_address severity (2026-10-15)

`transactions send` and `ipfs island subscribe` still defaulted `--fee`
to the string `"0.001"`, and the `transactions batch` per-entry fallback
did the same — a tenth of the chain standard fee. All three now import
and use `DEFAULT_TX_FEE_AIT` (0.01); the options show the default, and
the batch help/epilog plus the transaction-sending scenario doc stop
naming the stale value (the doc also claimed batch fee "defaults to
10.0"). `cli/tests/test_fee_defaults.py` proves default ==
`DEFAULT_TX_FEE_AIT` and explicit `--fee`/per-entry `fee` wins for all
three paths. The remaining `0.001` sites are non-signing (bridge
estimate's fake display data, exchange `min-order-size`, doc examples)
or explicit opt-in values (the MCP forwarding test); the staking and
wallet `--fee` options already agree at literal `0.01`.

`scripts/ops/exchange_verify.py` reported a registered `node_registry`
row without `eth_address` as an unconditional FAIL — but that is an
expected transitional state while `EXCHANGE_SETTLEMENT_ENABLED` is off
(a node can register before setting its payout address; the 15 Oct run
cost a director waiver). The flag lives in the exchange service env,
unreachable by an offline verifier whose own contract is "no environment
reads — all endpoints/addresses come from flags", so the script gains
`--settlement`: passed means the flag is on and the check tightens to
FAIL only when the node already has orders; unset (or the flag being
off) means payout is not yet needed and it is a WARN — mirroring the
service's own unset=off default. `aitbc exchange verify` forwards the
flag. Fixture-DB tests cover all three cases plus the
eth_address-present no-op; SQLite stays `mode=ro`.


